  limitations: "Fourteen machine-readable claims cover the twelve original integer/state APIs and two original rand_range trace contracts. Generic conversion and Kani results remain separate supplements; this is not full crate completion. All apply only in their recorded target/configuration and tool boundaries.\n\nRange proofs identify the exact sample and final state conditional on a finite first accepted candidate, and divergence for an all-rejected trace. They do not establish an accepted index exists for every state/range, general termination, uniformity, period or expected runtime. Debug assertion failure does not specify release behavior or Rust panic/unwinding effects. Save/restore is pure state identity, not a guarantee about arbitrary callbacks or destructors. No period, distribution, independence or cryptographic guarantee is established.\n\nThe external Kani checks do not prove global TypeId uniqueness, arbitrary CloneToUninit destinations or heap behavior: extent, alignment, nonoverlap and provenance obligations remain with the caller. Generic conversion contracts require actual trait instances and matching modeled dictionaries, preserve the selected associated error type, and do not establish arbitrary U has an instance. Callback correctness/termination, Drop/unwind/global effects, physical aliasing/layout, resource availability and const evaluation remain outside those contracts. Production source binding for the six generic families has not been added.\n\nEarlier direct combined float attempts timed out and are retained. The later Rand32 direct Z3 check succeeds; Rand64 direct Z3 still times out, while its successful two-stage composition establishes the stated contract. Other targets/configurations were not checked." -> "Twenty machine-readable claims cover fourteen original local APIs and six conditional external core conversion families. These six are not local declarations. Kani results remain separate supplements; this is not full crate completion. All apply only in their recorded target/configuration and tool boundaries.\n\nRange proofs identify the exact sample and final state conditional on a finite first accepted candidate, and divergence for an all-rejected trace. They do not establish an accepted index exists for every state/range, general termination, uniformity, period or expected runtime. Debug assertion failure does not specify release behavior or Rust panic/unwinding effects. Save/restore is pure state identity, not a guarantee about arbitrary callbacks or destructors. No period, distribution, independence or cryptographic guarantee is established.\n\nThe external Kani checks do not prove global TypeId uniqueness, arbitrary CloneToUninit destinations or heap behavior: extent, alignment, nonoverlap and provenance obligations remain with the caller. Generic conversion contracts require actual trait instances and matching modeled dictionaries, preserve the selected associated error type, and do not establish arbitrary U has an instance. Callback correctness/termination, Drop/unwind/global effects, physical aliasing/layout, resource availability and const evaluation remain outside those contracts. The six generic families use a finite compiler-owned source/body binding; arbitrary trait instances, implementations or configurations are not inferred.\n\nEarlier direct combined float attempts timed out and are retained. The later Rand32 direct Z3 check succeeds; Rand64 direct Z3 still times out, while its successful two-stage composition establishes the stated contract. Other targets/configurations were not checked."
